needClaims:
The direction under review is:

1. obtain an independent clean-clone reproduction of the current preview;
2. replace the arithmetic wiring fixture with a reviewed commitment and replay design;
3. make the typed `[binding]` manifest generate the Rust Type Script and adversarial test matrix;
4. pass a second, unrelated circuit through the same generator;
5. use a small Battleship game as a candidate reference application, binding each proof to the game, turn, move, and old-to-new state transition;
6. deploy the generated reference scripts to Pudge and publish accepted and rejected transaction evidence; and
7. broaden Noir support only through named, tested compatibility profiles.

Battleship is a proposed validation fixture, not a settled product decision. A better CKB-native use case may emerge from review.

I would like CKBuilders reviewers to review:

- whether the core concept fills a useful gap around CKB/Fiber application coordination;
- whether the protocol has unnecessary complexity;
- whether important replay, equivocation, crash-recovery, or retry cases are missing;
- whether the Fiber verification model is correct;
- whether the CKB checkpoint design makes sense;
- whether the adapter boundary is suitable for integration by other applications;
- whether there are existing CKB/Fiber projects or primitives with which EventMesh overlaps and should integrate instead of reimplementing;
- what would need to change before this could move from a Testnet reference implementation toward a production-capable system.

